```java
// Looks like it benchmarks string concatenation — measures nothing.
long start = System.nanoTime();
for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++) {
    String s = "a" + "b";   // result never used
}
long elapsed = System.nanoTime() - start;
```

An optimizing compiler or JIT can prove the loop body's result is never observed (`s` is never read after assignment) and eliminate the entire loop as dead code. The benchmark reports a suspiciously tiny, almost-zero elapsed time — and it's correct, because it genuinely did almost no work. The mistake isn't in the timer; it's in writing a benchmark whose output the optimizer is free to discard.

The fix is to force the result to be observed: accumulate results into a variable the benchmark returns or prints, or use a purpose-built micro-benchmark harness (JMH's `Blackhole`, Google Benchmark's `DoNotOptimize`) designed specifically to defeat this. A suspiciously fast benchmark result — one that seems too good given what the code does — is a signal to check for dead-code elimination before celebrating.
